<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <AnalysisMode>Recommended</AnalysisMode>

    <!-- Shared versioning: every package in this repository publishes this number, and no
         .csproj overrides it. Bump it here, commit to main, and the workflow publishes the whole
         set together.

         This is the point of the scheme rather than a side effect. `dotnet pack` turns each
         ProjectReference into a PackageReference at the referenced project's version, so a
         package that is not republished keeps pointing at whatever its dependency was on the day
         it was last packed. Publishing everything together means those floors are always current.
         Per-package numbers left cl2j.Logging depending on cl2j.FileStorage 2.0.0 while 2.2.0 was
         out, two data-loss fixes behind, with nothing to signal it — see issue #17.

         The cost, accepted knowingly: a package that did not change still gets a new number, so
         the version says which release a package belongs to, not what changed inside it.

         4.0.0 was the first shared number after the per-package period, clearing every version
         published during it — the highest being cl2j.Image 3.1.0.

         4.1.0: string keys bind as ANSI (#7). No API break, but a change in behaviour, hence the
         minor rather than the patch.

         4.1.1: CommandBuilderFactory no longer throws when a registration lands during a resolve
         (#15). A defect fix with no API or behaviour change for correct callers, hence the patch.

         5.0.0: the correctness pass on cl2j.Database (#25, #26, #27 and their neighbours). No
         signature changed, so nothing fails to compile — which is exactly why this is a major
         rather than a minor. A caller relying on the old behaviour keeps building and starts
         behaving differently:

           - results bind by column name, so a query whose column names do not match the type now
             throws where it used to return values by position;
           - a string key is written by INSERT instead of being dropped;
           - INSERT hands back the identity the server picked, where it returned null before;
           - CreateTable maps nullable value types, byte[], TimeSpan, DateOnly and Guid to real
             column types rather than varchar, and refuses a type it cannot map instead of falling
             back to varchar(MAX). A decimal must declare its precision;
           - TableExists no longer reads every failure as "the table does not exist", and DropTable
             no longer swallows a failed drop;
           - registering a provider twice replaces it rather than appending, so the later
             registration is the one that answers. It used to be the first, which made a custom
             IIdentifierGenerator passed on a second call silently do nothing.

         Existing tables are not migrated: CreateTableIfRequired checks existence, not shape.

         The same release also corrects three things in cl2j.Tooling (#31), two of which change
         what a caller sees:

           - a transliteration of more than one letter keeps all of it. "Ærøskøbing" was
             "Aroskobing" and is now "AEroskobing"; the four Cyrillic characters that transliterate
             to nothing threw IndexOutOfRangeException and now disappear as intended. Slugs built
             from a name containing æ, œ, ä, ö, ü, ß, ð or Cyrillic therefore change: check for
             published addresses before upgrading. RemoveDiacritics(char) returns a string, which
             is the one signature change in this release — the char it returned had nowhere to put
             the second letter;
           - a generic type argument that is itself generic is written with its namespace. This
             output goes straight into source that cl2j.Database and cl2j.Scripting compile, so
             the bare name was a compile error inside generated code;
           - Observable.NotifyAsync notifies each observer exactly once and throws an
             AggregateException listing what failed. It used to re-run the whole loop on any
             failure — performing other observers' side effects twice — and then swallow
             everything.

         And one in cl2j.DataStore (#32): DataStoreListCommandAndQueryArchive named its snapshots
         with the twelve-hour clock, so the 13:45 archive overwrote the 01:45 one — every day, for
         half of every day. Names are now on the 24-hour clock, which means existing archives keep
         theirs and new ones stop colliding.

         Two additions, neither of which breaks a caller: RestClient takes an HttpMessageHandler
         (so it can be tested, and so a handler can be shared the way IHttpClientFactory expects),
         and the archive decorator takes an optional clock.

         Finally, the five behaviour changes from #32 and #33 that were characterised rather than
         fixed. They are here deliberately: each one would have needed its own major release, and
         gathering them means the next one does not have to be. Three of the five are a compile
         error rather than a silent change, which is the kinder half.

           - GetAllAsync returns IReadOnlyDictionary / IReadOnlyList instead of the concrete types.
             It used to hand back the cache itself, so a caller that changed the result changed the
             cache — outside the semaphore, with no observer told. A read-only *view*, not a copy:
             wrapping is O(1), and consumers call this on the request path. The observable is typed
             the same way, so an IObserver<List<T>> becomes IObserver<IReadOnlyList<T>>. This is
             the compile break, and it reaches every implementation of IDataStoreDictionaryLoad and
             IDataStoreListLoad;
           - the dictionary cache copies what ReplaceAllByAsync is given rather than keeping the
             caller's own dictionary as the cache. The list cache already copied; they now agree;
           - an update whose key the cache has not seen is cached and announced rather than written
             to the store and dropped. A row that reached the store after the last refresh —
             another process, another instance — was exactly that case;
           - IsEmailValid uses \z rather than \Z, so an address with a trailing newline is refused.
             \Z matches before one;
           - EmailService catches only what a delivery attempt can produce (SmtpException,
             FormatException, IOException, SocketException, OperationCanceledException) instead of
             every exception. A defect in this class used to be indistinguishable from the server
             being down: both returned false. A malformed address is still a reported failure —
             that is data, not a defect.

         And the four that were left standing after those, so that nothing breaking is carried
         forward:

           - a cache serves whatever the store accepted on insert, instead of throwing on a key it
             already holds. A store that refuses duplicates refuses them itself, so the only case
             the old cache.Add ever caught was a store that upserts — and there it left the store
             holding the new value and the cache the old one;
           - the ordering predicate is applied after every write, not only at load. Anything
             written between two refreshes used to sit at the end, in an order that was neither the
             one asked for nor the store's;
           - GetAllAsync throws when the cache has never loaded, rather than answering "nothing".
             An unreadable source and an empty one were indistinguishable, and every caller read
             them as empty. A refresh that fails after a successful load is still caught and
             logged: a cache with data goes on serving it;
           - IEmailService returns EmailResult rather than bool, carrying the failure. A wrong
             password, an unreachable server and a rejected recipient used to be the same value,
             with the reason only in a log line. There is deliberately no implicit conversion to
             bool: the callers who most need to read Failure are the ones who would never look.

         Everything breaking was gathered here on purpose, so the next release does not have to be
         a major too. A minor would let all of it arrive through a routine bump. -->
    <Version>5.0.0</Version>
    <Authors>CL2J Technologies</Authors>
    <Company>CL2J Technologies</Company>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/CL2J-Technologies/dotnet-toolkit</RepositoryUrl>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>MIT</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <EditorConfigFiles Remove=".editorconfig" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
